question:
What is a dynamic block used for in Terraform?
```
dynamic "ingress" {
  for_each = var.ingress_rules
  content {
    from_port = ingress.value.port
    to_port   = ingress.value.port
    protocol  = "tcp"
  }
}
```

Given the code:
```
variable "users" {
  type = set(string)
}

resource "aws_iam_user" "u" {
  for_each = var.users
  name     = each.value
}
```
If var.users = ["alice", "bob"], what resource addresses will Terraform create?

Given the code with multiple resource instances via count:
```
resource "aws_instance" "web" {
  count = 3
  ...
}

output "all_ids" {
  value = aws_instance.web[*].id
}
```
What will the all_ids output return?

Given the code:
```
resource "aws_instance" "web" {
  ami           = "ami-12345"
  instance_type = "t2.micro"

  lifecycle {
    ignore_changes = [ami]
  }
}
```
What happens if the instance's real AMI is changed manually (not via Terraform), and then terraform plan is run?

What is the locals block used for in Terraform?
```
locals {
  full_name = "${var.project}-${var.environment}"
}
```

Given the code:
```
variable "instance_count" {
  type = number
  validation {
    condition     = var.instance_count > 0 && var.instance_count <= 10
    error_message = "instance_count must be between 1 and 10."
  }
}
```
What happens if instance_count = 15 is passed at apply time?

Given the code:
```
provider "aws" {
  region = "eu-west-1"
}

provider "aws" {
  alias  = "us_east"
  region = "us-east-1"
}

resource "aws_instance" "backup" {
  provider = aws.us_east
  ...
}
```
What is alias used for here?

Given the code (Terraform 1.3+):
```
variable "config" {
  type = object({
    name    = string
    timeout = optional(number, 30)
  })
}
```
What happens if the module is called with var.config but without the timeout field?

Starting with Terraform 1.5, a declarative import block was introduced:
```
import {
  to = aws_instance.web
  id = "i-0123456789"
}

resource "aws_instance" "web" {
  ami           = "ami-12345"
  instance_type = "t2.micro"
}
```
How does this differ from the terraform import command?

Given the code:
```
resource "random_id" "suffix" {
  byte_length = 4
}

resource "aws_s3_bucket" "data" {
  bucket = "my-app-data-${random_id.suffix.hex}"
}
```
Why is the random_id resource used here?

Given the code:
```
resource "null_resource" "deploy" {
  triggers = {
    code_hash = filesha256("app.zip")
  }

  provisioner "local-exec" {
    command = "./deploy.sh"
  }
}
```
Under what condition will the provisioner run again on the next terraform apply?

Given the code:
```
module "network" {
  source = "./modules/network"
}

module "app" {
  source     = "./modules/app"
  depends_on = [module.network]
}
```
Why is depends_on explicitly specified at the module level here?

Given the code:
```
variable "tags_by_team" {
  type = map(string)
}

resource "aws_instance" "srv" {
  for_each = var.tags_by_team
  ami      = "ami-12345"
  tags = {
    Team = each.key
    Env  = each.value
  }
}
```
What do each.key and each.value contain when iterating over a map variable in for_each?

Given the code:
```
resource "aws_instance" "web" {
  count = 3
  tags = {
    Name = "web-${count.index}"
  }
}
```
What values will the Name tag take on the three created instances?

Given the code:
```
locals {
  defaults = { size = "small", region = "eu-west-1" }
  override = { size = "large" }
  final    = merge(local.defaults, local.override)
}
```
What will local.final contain?

Given the code:
```
variable "custom_ami" {
  type    = string
  default = null
}

locals {
  ami = coalesce(var.custom_ami, data.aws_ami.default.id)
}
```
What does the coalesce() function do in this expression?

Given the code:
```
terraform {
  required_providers {
    aws = {
      source  = "hashicorp/aws"
      version = ">= 4.0, != 4.5.0"
    }
  }
}
```
Which provider versions does this constraint allow?

Given the code:
```
variable "server" {
  type = object({
    name = string
    cpu  = number
  })
}
```
How does object({...}) differ from map(string)?

Given the code:
```
variable "names" {
  type = list(string)
}

locals {
  upper_names = [for n in var.names : upper(n)]
}
```
What is the [for n in var.names : upper(n)] construct?

Given the code:
```
variable "instances" {
  type = list(object({ name = string, size = string }))
}

locals {
  by_name = { for i in var.instances : i.name => i.size }
}
```
What does this for expression with curly braces ({ for ... : key => value }) return?

Given the code:
```
variable "servers" {
  type = list(object({ name = string, enabled = bool }))
}

locals {
  enabled_names = [for s in var.servers : s.name if s.enabled]
}
```
What does the "if s.enabled" part do in this for expression?

Given the code:
```
data "aws_ami" "latest" {
  most_recent = true
  owners      = ["self"]
}

resource "aws_instance" "web" {
  ami        = data.aws_ami.latest.id
  depends_on = [aws_iam_role_policy.image_read]
}
```
Why might depends_on referencing an IAM policy be needed here, even though the aws_ami data source doesn't reference it directly?

Given the code with nested modules:
```
# root module
module "network" {
  source = "./modules/network"
}

# modules/network/main.tf
module "subnets" {
  source = "./subnets"
}
```
What is the address of a resource from the subnets module, nested inside the network module, when viewed from the root module (e.g. in terraform state list)?

Given the code:
```
# modules/app/main.tf
terraform {
  required_providers {
    aws = {
      source                = "hashicorp/aws"
      configuration_aliases = [aws.euwest]
    }
  }
}

resource "aws_instance" "web" {
  provider = aws.euwest
  ...
}
```
What is configuration_aliases used for in the module?

Given the code:
```
resource "aws_db_instance" "main" {
  identifier         = "prod-db"
  engine             = "postgres"
  skip_final_snapshot = false
  final_snapshot_identifier = "prod-db-final"

  lifecycle {
    prevent_destroy = true
  }
}
```
What happens if you try to run terraform destroy (or change the configuration so this resource must be destroyed)?

Given the code:
```
resource "aws_instance" "web" {
  ami = "ami-12345"
  tags = {
    DeployedAt = timestamp()
  }
}
```
Why does using timestamp() directly in a resource tag cause a constant diff on every terraform plan (similar to the previously discussed uuid() problem)?

Given the code:
```
resource "aws_instance" "web" {
  ami = "ami-12345"
}

resource "aws_eip" "ip" {
  instance = aws_instance.web.id
}

output "server_ip" {
  value = aws_eip.ip.public_ip
}
```
Why can't the value of the server_ip output be known before terraform apply, even if you run terraform plan?

Given the code:
```
terraform {
  backend "s3" {
    bucket = "my-tf-state"
    key    = "env/${terraform.workspace}/terraform.tfstate"
    region = "eu-west-1"
  }
}
```
Will this code work as intended (a different state path for different CLI workspaces)?

Given the code:
```
locals {
  config_yaml = yamlencode({
    replicas = 3
    image    = "nginx:1.25"
  })
}
```
What could the result of yamlencode() be used for in real Terraform code (for example, when working with Kubernetes manifests)?

Given the code:
```
variable "ports" {
  type    = list(number)
  default = [443, 22, 8080, 80]
}

locals {
  sorted_ports = sort(var.ports)
}
```
What does sort(var.ports) return for the list of numbers [443, 22, 8080, 80]? And how does sort() behave with lists of strings?

Given the code:
```
locals {
  server = { name = "web-01", region = "eu-west-1", size = "t3.micro" }
  field_names  = keys(local.server)
  field_values = values(local.server)
}
```
What will keys(local.server) and values(local.server) return?

Given the code:
```
variable "checks" {
  type = object({
    has_backup   = bool
    has_monitoring = bool
    has_alerts   = bool
  })
}

locals {
  fully_compliant = alltrue([var.checks.has_backup, var.checks.has_monitoring, var.checks.has_alerts])
}
```
What does the alltrue() function do in this expression?

Given the code with an external data source:
```
data "external" "git_info" {
  program = ["python3", "${path.module}/scripts/get_git_sha.py"]
}

locals {
  commit_sha = data.external.git_info.result.sha
}
```
What is the external data source intended for, and what requirement is placed on the invoked program?

Given the code:
```
data "http" "latest_release" {
  url = "https://api.github.com/repos/hashicorp/terraform/releases/latest"
}

locals {
  latest_version = jsondecode(data.http.latest_release.response_body).tag_name
}
```
What does the http data source do in this code?

Given the code (Terraform 1.4+):
```
resource "terraform_data" "deployment_trigger" {
  input = var.app_version

  provisioner "local-exec" {
    command = "echo Deploying version ${self.input}"
  }
}
```
How does the terraform_data resource differ from the older null_resource, which was used for similar purposes?

Given the code:
```
resource "null_resource" "deploy" {
  triggers = {
    app_hash    = filemd5("${path.module}/app.zip")
    config_hash = filemd5("${path.module}/config.json")
  }

  provisioner "local-exec" {
    command = "./deploy.sh"
  }
}
```
Under what condition will the local-exec provisioner inside this null_resource run again on the next terraform apply?

Given the code with a provisioner:
```
resource "aws_instance" "web" {
  ami = "ami-12345"

  provisioner "remote-exec" {
    inline = [
      "echo 'Instance ID: ${self.id}'",
      "echo 'Public IP: ${self.public_ip}'"
    ]
  }
}
```
What does the self keyword mean in the context of a provisioner block?

Given the code:
```
resource "aws_instance" "web" {
  ami           = "ami-12345"
  instance_type = "t2.micro"

  lifecycle {
    ignore_changes = all
  }
}
```
What is the difference between ignore_changes = all and specifying a concrete list of attributes, e.g., ignore_changes = [tags, instance_type]?

Given the code:
```
resource "aws_instance" "web" {
  count = var.enable_bastion ? 1 : 0
  ami   = "ami-12345"
}

output "bastion_ip" {
  value = one(aws_instance.web[*].public_ip)
}
```
Why is the one() function used here in combination with the splat expression aws_instance.web[*].public_ip?

Given the code:
```
data "terraform_remote_state" "shared" {
  backend = "remote"
  config = {
    organization = "my-org"
    workspaces = {
      name = "networking-prod"
    }
  }
}
```
What happens if the workspaces block is omitted entirely (for a backend of type "remote", used with HCP Terraform)?

Given the code:
```
locals {
  build_time = timestamp()
}

resource "null_resource" "deploy" {
  triggers = {
    always = local.build_time
  }
}
```
Which characteristic of the timestamp() function is important for understanding this code's behavior across repeated terraform plan runs?

Given the code:
```
variable "custom_ami" {
  type    = string
  default = null
}

locals {
  ami = coalesce(var.custom_ami, data.aws_ami.default.id)
}
```
How does coalesce() behave if var.custom_ami is not set (equals null)?

Given the code:
```
variable "db_password" {
  type      = string
  sensitive = true
}

output "connection_string" {
  value = nonsensitive("postgres://user:${var.db_password}@host/db")
}
```
What happens during terraform apply?

Given the code:
```
resource "aws_db_instance" "main" {
  # ...
  password = var.db_password
}

variable "db_password" {
  type      = string
  sensitive = true
}
```
If the password argument of the aws_db_instance resource itself is also declared sensitive by the provider, which of the following INCORRECTLY describes sensitive's effect on the actual security of the value?

Given the code:
```
resource "aws_security_group" "web" {
  name = "web-sg"

  dynamic "ingress" {
    for_each = var.allowed_ports
    content {
      from_port   = ingress.value
      to_port     = ingress.value
      protocol    = "tcp"
      cidr_blocks = ["0.0.0.0/0"]
    }
  }
}
```
What does the dynamic "ingress" block do in this example?

Given the code:
```
dynamic "tag" {
  for_each = var.extra_tags
  iterator = t
  content {
    key   = t.key
    value = t.value
  }
}
```
What is the purpose of the iterator = t attribute inside this dynamic block?

Given the code:
```
variable "instance_config" {
  type = object({
    size     = string
    monitoring = optional(bool, false)
  })
}
```
What happens if the calling code passes `instance_config = { size = "large" }`, without specifying monitoring at all?

Given the code:
```
variable "environment" {
  type = string

  validation {
    condition     = contains(["dev", "staging", "prod"], var.environment)
    error_message = "environment must be one of: dev, staging, prod."
  }
}
```
At what stage will this check run if the user passes environment = "production" (a typo)?

Given the code:
```
variable "port" {
  type = number

  validation {
    condition     = var.port > 0
    error_message = "port must be positive."
  }

  validation {
    condition     = var.port <= 65535
    error_message = "port must be <= 65535."
  }
}
```
Can a single variable have MULTIPLE validation blocks, and how do they interact?

Given the code:
```
resource "aws_instance" "web" {
  ami           = var.ami_id
  instance_type = var.instance_type

  lifecycle {
    precondition {
      condition     = data.aws_ami.selected.architecture == "x86_64"
      error_message = "Selected AMI must be x86_64 architecture."
    }
  }
}
```
What is the difference between a precondition inside lifecycle and a validation inside variable?

Given the code:
```
resource "aws_instance" "web" {
  # ...
  lifecycle {
    postcondition {
      condition     = self.public_ip != ""
      error_message = "Instance must have a public IP assigned."
    }
  }
}
```
When is a postcondition checked, and what does self mean in this context?

Given the code:
```
check "health_check" {
  data "http" "api" {
    url = "https://api.example.com/health"
  }

  assert {
    condition     = data.http.api.status_code == 200
    error_message = "API health check failed."
  }
}
```
How does a check block affect the overall result of terraform plan/apply if assert.condition turns out to be false?

Given the code:
```
moved {
  from = aws_instance.old_name
  to   = aws_instance.new_name
}

resource "aws_instance" "new_name" {
  # ...
}
```
How does a moved block (introduced in Terraform 1.1) differ from manually running `terraform state mv` beforehand?

Given the code:
```
removed {
  from = aws_instance.legacy

  lifecycle {
    destroy = false
  }
}
```
What happens to the aws_instance.legacy resource on the next apply, after the corresponding resource block has been removed from the code?

Given the code:
```
import {
  to = aws_instance.web
  id = "i-0abcd1234ef567890"
}

resource "aws_instance" "web" {
  ami           = "ami-12345"
  instance_type = "t3.micro"
}
```
How does an import block (Terraform 1.5+) differ from the `terraform import` CLI command?
